package aws

import "github.com/rusik69/lc/internal/problems"

func init() {
	problems.RegisterAWSQuestions([]problems.Question{
		{ID: 1, Text: "What does AWS stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Amazon Web Services", "Amazon Warehouse Systems", "Advanced Web Solutions", "Automated Workflow Services"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AWS stands for Amazon Web Services, Amazon's comprehensive cloud computing platform. Provides Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), Platform as a Service (PaaS), and Software as a Service (SaaS). Offers 200+ services including compute, storage, databases, networking, analytics, AI/ML. Largest cloud provider globally. Pay-as-you-go pricing model."},
		{ID: 2, Text: "What does EC2 stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Elastic Container Cloud", "Elastic Compute Cloud", "Enhanced Computing Cluster", "Elastic Cache Cloud"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EC2 stands for Elastic Compute Cloud, AWS's service that provides resizable virtual servers in the cloud. Choose instance types, operating systems, storage. Scale up/down based on demand. Pay only for what you use. Core AWS compute service. Supports various instance types (general purpose, compute optimized, memory optimized, etc.)."},
		{ID: 3, Text: "What type of storage is S3?", Options: [4]string{"Block storage", "Object storage", "File storage", "Database storage"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "S3 (Simple Storage Service) is AWS's object storage service. Stores data as objects in buckets. Highly scalable (unlimited storage), durable (99.999999999% durability), available. Supports versioning, encryption, lifecycle policies. Used for backups, static websites, data lakes, application data. Core AWS storage service."},
		{ID: 4, Text: "Which S3 storage class is cheapest for archival data?", Options: [4]string{"S3 Standard", "S3 Intelligent-Tiering", "S3 Standard-IA", "S3 Glacier Deep Archive"}, CorrectAnswer: 3, Explanation: "S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the cheapest storage class for long-term archival data. S3 storage classes are different storage tiers optimized for different access patterns. Standard (frequent access), Standard-IA (infrequent access, lower cost), Glacier (archival, very low cost), Intelligent-Tiering (automatic optimization). Choose based on access frequency and cost requirements."},
		{ID: 5, Text: "What type of databases does RDS support?", Options: [4]string{"Only NoSQL databases", "Relational databases", "Graph databases", "Time-series databases"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "RDS (Relational Database Service) is AWS's managed relational database service. Supports MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server. Handles backups, patching, monitoring, scaling automatically. Multi-AZ for high availability, read replicas for scaling. Simplifies database administration. Focus on application, not database management."},
		{ID: 6, Text: "What is the billing model for Lambda?", Options: [4]string{"Pay per hour", "Pay per request and execution time", "Pay per provisioned capacity", "Flat monthly fee"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Lambda charges per request and per execution time consumed. Lambda is AWS's serverless compute service. Run code without provisioning servers. Event-driven - triggered by events (API calls, file uploads, schedule, etc.). Supports multiple languages. Auto-scales. Ideal for microservices, data processing, automation. No server management required."},
		{ID: 7, Text: "What does VPC stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Virtual Private Cloud", "Virtual Public Container", "Verified Private Cluster", "Virtual Processing Core"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "VPC stands for Virtual Private Cloud, which provides an isolated virtual network in AWS. Define your own IP address range, subnets, route tables, gateways. Control network access with security groups and NACLs. Connect to on-premises via VPN or Direct Connect. Foundation for secure AWS networking. Default VPC provided, but custom VPCs recommended."},
		{ID: 8, Text: "What format are IAM policies written in?", Options: [4]string{"XML", "JSON", "YAML", "TOML"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "IAM policies are written in JSON format. IAM (Identity and Access Management) controls access to AWS resources. Create users, groups, roles. Define permissions via policies. Principle of least privilege. MFA support. Access keys for programmatic access. Root account should be protected. Essential for AWS security. Centralized access control."},
		{ID: 9, Text: "What formats does CloudFormation support for templates?", Options: [4]string{"Only JSON", "Only YAML", "YAML or JSON", "XML or JSON"}, CorrectAnswer: 2, Explanation: "CloudFormation supports both YAML and JSON formats for templates. CloudFormation enables Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Define infrastructure in templates. Automatically provisions and manages AWS resources. Ensures consistency, repeatability. Version control infrastructure. Stack-based - create, update, delete entire stacks. Essential for DevOps and infrastructure automation."},
		{ID: 10, Text: "What is CloudWatch primarily used for?", Options: [4]string{"Code deployment", "Monitoring and observability", "Database management", "Identity management"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "CloudWatch is AWS's monitoring and observability service. Collects metrics from AWS services and applications. Stores and analyzes logs. Sets alarms and triggers actions. Dashboards for visualization. Integrated with most AWS services. Essential for monitoring AWS resources and applications. Helps maintain system health and performance."},
		{ID: 11, Text: "What type of service is Route 53?", Options: [4]string{"Load balancing service", "DNS service", "CDN service", "Storage service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Route 53 is AWS's DNS (Domain Name System) service. Manages domain registration and DNS resolution. Routes traffic to AWS resources based on health checks. Supports various routing policies (simple, weighted, latency-based, geolocation, failover). Integrates with other AWS services. Essential for domain management and traffic routing."},
		{ID: 12, Text: "Which load balancer operates at Layer 7?", Options: [4]string{"Network Load Balancer", "Application Load Balancer", "Classic Load Balancer", "Gateway Load Balancer"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Application Load Balancer (ALB) operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS). ELB (Elastic Load Balancer) distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets (EC2 instances, containers, IPs). Types: ALB (Layer 7), Network Load Balancer (NLB, Layer 4), Classic Load Balancer. Provides high availability, automatic scaling, health checks. Essential for distributing load."},
		{ID: 13, Text: "What metrics can trigger Auto Scaling?", Options: [4]string{"Only CPU utilization", "Only memory utilization", "CPU, memory, or custom metrics", "Only network throughput"}, CorrectAnswer: 2, Explanation: "Auto Scaling can trigger based on CPU, memory, custom metrics, or schedules. Auto Scaling automatically adjusts capacity to maintain performance and minimize costs. Scales EC2 instances based on demand. Maintains desired capacity. Works with ELB for load distribution. Essential for handling variable workloads efficiently."},
		{ID: 14, Text: "What type of storage is EBS?", Options: [4]string{"Object storage", "Block storage", "File storage", "Archive storage"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EBS (Elastic Block Store) provides persistent block storage volumes for EC2 instances. Like virtual hard drives. Types: gp3/gp2 (general purpose), io1/io2 (provisioned IOPS), st1 (throughput optimized), sc1 (cold HDD). Can be attached/detached, backed up with snapshots. Essential for EC2 persistent storage."},
		{ID: 15, Text: "What protocol does EFS use?", Options: [4]string{"SMB", "NFS", "iSCSI", "FTP"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EFS (Elastic File System) uses NFS (Network File System) protocol for EC2 instances. Shared file storage accessible by multiple instances simultaneously. Scales automatically. Pay for storage used. Supports EFS Standard and EFS Infrequent Access. Ideal for shared data, content management, web serving. Serverless - no provisioning needed."},
		{ID: 16, Text: "What type of database is DynamoDB?", Options: [4]string{"Relational database", "NoSQL database", "Graph database", "Time-series database"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "DynamoDB is AWS's fully managed NoSQL database. Key-value and document database. Single-digit millisecond latency. Auto-scaling, built-in security, backups. Serverless - no servers to manage. Pay for what you use. Supports on-demand and provisioned capacity. Ideal for applications requiring consistent performance at scale."},
		{ID: 17, Text: "What does SQS stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Simple Query Service", "Simple Queue Service", "Secure Queue System", "Standard Query Service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "SQS stands for Simple Queue Service, a fully managed message queuing service. Decouples and scales microservices, distributed systems, serverless applications. Two types: Standard (at-least-once delivery) and FIFO (exactly-once, ordered). Pull-based messaging. Essential for building scalable, resilient applications. Enables asynchronous processing."},
		{ID: 18, Text: "What messaging pattern does SNS use?", Options: [4]string{"Point-to-point", "Pub/sub", "Request-reply", "Broadcast"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "SNS (Simple Notification Service) uses pub/sub (publish-subscribe) messaging pattern. Sends notifications to multiple subscribers (email, SMS, SQS, Lambda, HTTP). Push-based messaging. Topics for grouping subscribers. Supports filtering. Essential for event-driven architectures and notifications. Decouples publishers and subscribers."},
		{ID: 19, Text: "What is CloudFront?", Options: [4]string{"Database service", "Content Delivery Network", "Compute service", "Storage service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "CloudFront is AWS's Content Delivery Network (CDN). Caches content at edge locations worldwide for low latency. Delivers static and dynamic content. Integrates with S3, EC2, ELB, custom origins. Supports SSL/TLS, custom domains, signed URLs. Reduces load on origin servers. Essential for global content delivery and performance."},
		{ID: 20, Text: "What types of APIs does API Gateway support?", Options: [4]string{"Only REST APIs", "Only WebSocket APIs", "REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs", "Only GraphQL APIs"}, CorrectAnswer: 2, Explanation: "API Gateway supports REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs. API Gateway is a fully managed service for creating, publishing, and managing APIs. Integrates with Lambda, EC2, other AWS services. Handles authentication, throttling, caching, monitoring. Pay per API call. Essential for building serverless APIs and microservices. Provides API versioning and deployment stages."},
		{ID: 21, Text: "What does ECS stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Elastic Compute Service", "Elastic Container Service", "Enhanced Cloud Service", "Elastic Cluster Service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "ECS stands for Elastic Container Service, AWS's container orchestration service. Runs Docker containers on EC2 or Fargate (serverless). Manages container lifecycle, scaling, load balancing. Two launch types: EC2 (manage infrastructure) and Fargate (serverless). Integrates with ALB, CloudWatch, IAM. Essential for containerized applications on AWS."},
		{ID: 22, Text: "What container orchestration technology does EKS use?", Options: [4]string{"Docker Swarm", "Kubernetes", "Apache Mesos", "Nomad"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) uses Kubernetes as its container orchestration technology. Runs Kubernetes clusters without managing control plane. Compatible with standard Kubernetes tools. Integrates with AWS services (IAM, VPC, ELB). Handles control plane availability and updates. Essential for Kubernetes workloads on AWS. Supports both EC2 and Fargate nodes."},
		{ID: 23, Text: "What does ECR stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Elastic Compute Registry", "Elastic Container Registry", "Enhanced Cloud Repository", "Elastic Cluster Registry"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "ECR stands for Elastic Container Registry, AWS's Docker container registry. Stores and manages Docker images. Integrated with ECS and EKS. Supports image scanning for vulnerabilities. Private registry with IAM access control. Pay for storage and data transfer. Essential for container image management on AWS."},
		{ID: 24, Text: "What does CI/CD stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery", "Cloud Infrastructure/Cloud Deployment", "Container Integration/Container Deployment", "Code Integration/Code Delivery"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (or Deployment). CodePipeline is AWS's CI/CD service. Automates build, test, and deployment pipelines. Integrates with CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, GitHub, S3. Visual pipeline editor. Supports multiple stages and parallel actions. Pay per pipeline execution. Essential for DevOps automation."},
		{ID: 25, Text: "What is CodeBuild primarily used for?", Options: [4]string{"Deploying applications", "Compiling source code and running tests", "Managing infrastructure", "Monitoring applications"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "CodeBuild is primarily used for compiling source code and running tests. CodeBuild is AWS's fully managed build service. Scales automatically - no servers to manage. Supports multiple languages and build environments. Pay for compute time used. Integrates with CodePipeline. Essential for CI/CD pipelines. Handles build dependencies automatically."},
		{ID: 26, Text: "What deployment strategy minimizes downtime by running two environments?", Options: [4]string{"Rolling deployment", "Blue/green deployment", "Canary deployment", "In-place deployment"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Blue/green deployment minimizes downtime by maintaining two identical environments (blue and green). CodeDeploy automates application deployments to EC2, Lambda, ECS, on-premises. Supports rolling, blue/green, and in-place deployments. Automatic rollback on failure. Integrates with CodePipeline. Essential for reliable deployments. Handles deployment complexity automatically."},
		{ID: 27, Text: "Which AWS service automatically rotates database credentials?", Options: [4]string{"Systems Manager Parameter Store", "Secrets Manager", "KMS", "CloudHSM"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Secrets Manager automatically rotates secrets for RDS, Redshift, and DocumentDB. Stores and manages secrets (passwords, API keys, tokens). Encrypted at rest and in transit. Integrates with IAM for access control. Pay per secret per month. Replaces hardcoded credentials. Essential for secure credential management. Better than Parameter Store for secrets requiring rotation."},
		{ID: 28, Text: "What does KMS stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Kubernetes Management Service", "Key Management Service", "Knowledge Management System", "Kinesis Management Service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "KMS stands for Key Management Service. Creates and manages encryption keys. Customer Master Keys (CMKs) for encrypting data. Integrated with many AWS services (S3, EBS, RDS, etc.). Hardware Security Modules (HSM) for key storage. Supports key rotation. Essential for data encryption. Enables encryption at rest and in transit."},
		{ID: 29, Text: "What layer does WAF operate at?", Options: [4]string{"Layer 3 (Network)", "Layer 4 (Transport)", "Layer 7 (Application)", "Layer 2 (Data Link)"}, CorrectAnswer: 2, Explanation: "WAF (Web Application Firewall) operates at Layer 7 (Application layer). Protects web applications from common exploits. Filters HTTP/HTTPS traffic. Rules for SQL injection, XSS, rate limiting. Integrates with CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway. Custom rules or managed rule sets. Pay per rule per web ACL. Essential for web application security."},
		{ID: 30, Text: "What type of attack does Shield protect against?", Options: [4]string{"SQL injection", "DDoS attacks", "Cross-site scripting", "Man-in-the-middle"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Shield provides DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) protection. Shield Standard: free, always-on protection. Shield Advanced: enhanced protection with 24/7 support, cost protection, advanced mitigation. Protects EC2, ELB, CloudFront, Route 53, Global Accelerator. Automatic detection and mitigation. Essential for protecting against DDoS attacks."},
		{ID: 31, Text: "What technology does GuardDuty use for threat detection?", Options: [4]string{"Rule-based detection", "Machine learning", "Signature-based detection", "Manual analysis"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "GuardDuty uses machine learning for threat detection. Analyzes VPC Flow Logs, CloudTrail, DNS logs. Detects compromised instances, malicious activity, unauthorized access. Pay per GB of data analyzed. Continuous monitoring. Sends findings to CloudWatch Events. Essential for security monitoring. No infrastructure to manage."},
		{ID: 32, Text: "What does Inspector scan for vulnerabilities?", Options: [4]string{"Only S3 buckets", "EC2 instances and ECR images", "Only Lambda functions", "Only RDS databases"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Inspector scans EC2 instances and ECR images for vulnerabilities. Performs automated security assessments. Uses knowledge base of security best practices. Generates reports with findings and recommendations. Can be scheduled or triggered. Helps maintain security posture. Essential for vulnerability management. Identifies security issues automatically."},
		{ID: 33, Text: "What is AWS Config primarily used for?", Options: [4]string{"Application deployment", "Configuration management and compliance", "Code building", "Load balancing"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "AWS Config is primarily used for configuration management and compliance tracking. Records configuration history. Evaluates configurations against rules. Sends notifications on non-compliance. Provides configuration snapshots. Essential for compliance and auditing. Helps maintain desired configuration state. Integrates with CloudFormation and other services."},
		{ID: 34, Text: "Which Systems Manager feature stores configuration data?", Options: [4]string{"Run Command", "Parameter Store", "Session Manager", "Patch Manager"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Parameter Store is the Systems Manager feature that stores configuration data and secrets. Systems Manager provides operations management capabilities. Patch Manager for patching EC2 instances. Session Manager for secure shell access. Run Command for remote command execution. Automation for operational tasks. Essential for managing EC2 instances at scale."},
		{ID: 35, Text: "What does CloudTrail log?", Options: [4]string{"Application logs", "API calls to AWS services", "Database queries", "Network traffic"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "CloudTrail logs all API calls made to AWS services. Provides audit trail of who did what, when, from where. Logs stored in S3. Can be analyzed with CloudWatch Logs Insights or Athena. Essential for security auditing and compliance. Tracks all management and data events. Enables governance and compliance."},
		{ID: 36, Text: "What is X-Ray primarily used for?", Options: [4]string{"Cost optimization", "Distributed tracing and debugging", "Security scanning", "Data backup"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "X-Ray is primarily used for distributed tracing and debugging applications. Visualizes request flow through services. Identifies bottlenecks and errors. Service map shows dependencies. Trace analysis for debugging. Integrates with EC2, Lambda, ECS, ELB. Essential for debugging distributed applications. Helps understand application behavior and performance."},
		{ID: 37, Text: "What language does Step Functions use to define workflows?", Options: [4]string{"Python", "Amazon States Language (JSON)", "YAML", "JavaScript"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Step Functions uses Amazon States Language (JSON format) to define workflows. Orchestrates serverless workflows using state machines. Coordinates Lambda functions, ECS tasks, and other services. Visual workflow designer. Handles errors, retries, parallel execution. Pay per state transition. Essential for complex serverless workflows."},
		{ID: 38, Text: "What does EventBridge replace?", Options: [4]string{"CloudWatch Logs", "CloudWatch Events", "CloudTrail", "Config"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EventBridge replaces CloudWatch Events with enhanced functionality. EventBridge is a serverless event bus. Routes events from AWS services, SaaS applications, custom applications. Event-driven architecture. Rules route events to targets (Lambda, SQS, SNS, etc.). Schema registry for event schemas. Essential for event-driven applications. Decouples event producers and consumers."},
		{ID: 39, Text: "What does SES stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Simple Execution Service", "Simple Email Service", "Secure Email System", "Standard Encryption Service"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "SES stands for Simple Email Service, AWS's email sending service. Send transactional emails, marketing emails, notifications. High deliverability. Pay for emails sent. Supports email templates, bounces, complaints handling. Can receive emails (with S3/SNS). Essential for application email functionality. Cost-effective email delivery."},
		{ID: 40, Text: "What is the cheapest S3 storage class?", Options: [4]string{"S3 Standard", "S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval", "S3 Glacier Deep Archive", "S3 Standard-IA"}, CorrectAnswer: 2, Explanation: "S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the cheapest S3 storage class for long-term data archival. S3 Glacier is archival storage for long-term data retention. Very low cost storage. Retrieval times: Expedited (1-5 min), Standard (3-5 hours), Bulk (5-12 hours). Ideal for backups, archives, compliance data. Three tiers: Glacier Instant Retrieval, Glacier Flexible Retrieval, Glacier Deep Archive."},
		{ID: 41, Text: "What type of database workload is Redshift optimized for?", Options: [4]string{"OLTP (Online Transaction Processing)", "OLAP (Online Analytical Processing)", "NoSQL", "Graph databases"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Redshift is optimized for OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) workloads. Redshift is AWS's fully managed data warehouse. Petabyte-scale analytics database. Columnar storage for fast queries. SQL-based. Integrates with BI tools. Supports data loading from S3, ETL tools. Auto-scaling and backups. Essential for business intelligence and analytics."},
		{ID: 42, Text: "What query language does Athena use?", Options: [4]string{"MongoDB Query Language", "Standard SQL", "GraphQL", "Cypher"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "Athena uses standard SQL to query data. Athena is a serverless interactive query service. Query data in S3 using SQL. No infrastructure to manage. Pay per query per TB scanned. Works with various data formats (CSV, JSON, Parquet, etc.). Essential for ad-hoc analytics on S3 data. Fast and cost-effective for querying data lakes."},
		{ID: 43, Text: "What big data framework does EMR support?", Options: [4]string{"Only Hadoop", "Hadoop, Spark, HBase, Presto", "Only Apache Spark", "Only Apache Flink"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "EMR (Elastic MapReduce) supports Hadoop, Spark, HBase, Presto, and other big data frameworks. Processes large datasets. Auto-scales clusters. Pay for compute time. Integrates with S3, DynamoDB, other AWS services. Essential for big data processing and analytics. Handles petabyte-scale data processing."},
		{ID: 44, Text: "What does ETL stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Extract, Transfer, Load", "Extract, Transform, Load", "Export, Transform, Link", "Execute, Test, Launch"}, CorrectAnswer: 1, Explanation: "ETL stands for Extract, Transform, Load. Glue is a serverless ETL service. Prepares and transforms data for analytics. Glue Data Catalog: metadata repository (like Hive metastore). Glue ETL: serverless Spark jobs. Auto-generates ETL code. Pay for compute time. Essential for data preparation and ETL pipelines. Integrates with many data sources."},
		{ID: 45, Text: "What type of data does Kinesis primarily handle?", Options: [4]string{"Streaming data", "Batch data", "Static archives", "Database backups"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Kinesis is AWS's platform for streaming data. Kinesis Data Streams: real-time streaming data. Kinesis Data Firehose: load streams into S3, Redshift, etc. Kinesis Data Analytics: analyze streams with SQL. Handles millions of events per second. Essential for real-time data processing. Enables real-time analytics and processing."},
		{ID: 46, Text: "What are the two main components of Cognito?", Options: [4]string{"User Pools and Identity Pools", "Access Keys and Secret Keys", "Roles and Policies", "Groups and Permissions"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Cognito provides user authentication and identity management. User Pools: user directory and authentication. Identity Pools: federated identities (access AWS resources). Supports social identity providers (Google, Facebook, etc.). MFA support. Essential for mobile and web applications. Handles user sign-up, sign-in, password management."},
		{ID: 47, Text: "What type of connection does Direct Connect provide?", Options: [4]string{"Dedicated physical network connection", "VPN over internet", "Public internet connection", "Wireless satellite link"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Direct Connect establishes dedicated network connection from on-premises to AWS. Bypasses internet for more consistent performance. Lower latency, higher throughput. Private connectivity. Can reduce bandwidth costs. Supports 1Gbps and 10Gbps connections. Essential for hybrid cloud architectures. More reliable than VPN for production workloads."},
		{ID: 48, Text: "What protocol does AWS Site-to-Site VPN use?", Options: [4]string{"IPsec", "PPTP", "L2TP", "WireGuard"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AWS VPN provides secure connections to VPCs. Site-to-Site VPN: connect on-premises network to VPC. Client VPN: connect individual devices to VPC. Encrypted over internet. Lower cost than Direct Connect but less reliable. Uses IPsec. Essential for secure remote access. Good for development, testing, or low-bandwidth production."},
		{ID: 49, Text: "What network architecture pattern does Transit Gateway implement?", Options: [4]string{"Hub-and-spoke", "Mesh", "Ring", "Star"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Transit Gateway is a network transit hub. Connects VPCs, VPNs, Direct Connect in a central hub. Simplifies network architecture. Centralized routing. Reduces peering complexity. Supports route tables for segmentation. Pay per attachment and data processed. Essential for complex multi-VPC architectures. Hub-and-spoke model."},
		{ID: 50, Text: "What is created when you launch a CloudFormation template?", Options: [4]string{"A stack", "A cluster", "A pod", "A container"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A CloudFormation stack is a collection of AWS resources created from a template. Single unit of management - create, update, delete together. Tracks dependencies between resources. Rollback on failure. Stack events show progress. Can have nested stacks. Essential for infrastructure management. Represents a complete application or environment."},
		{ID: 51, Text: "What does an AWS region contain?", Options: [4]string{"Multiple Availability Zones", "A single data center", "Only edge locations", "Customer premises equipment"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A region is a geographic area containing multiple Availability Zones. Each region is isolated and independent. Choose regions based on latency, compliance, data residency. Resources are region-specific (not global). Examples: us-east-1, eu-west-1, ap-southeast-1. Essential for global deployments and compliance."},
		{ID: 52, Text: "How are Availability Zones within a region connected?", Options: [4]string{"Low-latency links", "Public internet", "Satellite connections", "No direct connection"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "An Availability Zone (AZ) is one or more discrete data centers in a region. Isolated from failures in other AZs. Connected via low-latency links. Deploy across multiple AZs for high availability. Each region has 2-6 AZs. Essential for fault tolerance and disaster recovery."},
		{ID: 53, Text: "What is the primary benefit of Multi-AZ deployment?", Options: [4]string{"High availability", "Cost reduction", "Faster performance", "Increased storage"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Multi-AZ deployment runs resources across multiple Availability Zones. Provides high availability - if one AZ fails, resources in other AZs continue. Automatic failover for RDS, ElastiCache. Reduces downtime risk. Essential for production workloads. Increases availability from ~99.5% to 99.95%+."},
		{ID: 54, Text: "Are security groups stateful or stateless?", Options: [4]string{"Stateful", "Stateless", "Hybrid", "Neither"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A security group is a virtual firewall for EC2 instances. Controls inbound and outbound traffic. Stateful - return traffic automatically allowed. Default: deny all inbound, allow all outbound. Can reference other security groups. Essential for network security. Applied at instance level."},
		{ID: 55, Text: "At what level does a NACL apply?", Options: [4]string{"Subnet level", "Instance level", "VPC level", "Region level"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "NACL (Network Access Control List) is a subnet-level firewall. Stateless - must allow both directions explicitly. Rules evaluated in order (lowest number first). Default NACL allows all traffic. More granular than security groups. Essential for subnet-level network control. Applied at subnet level."},
		{ID: 56, Text: "What type of credentials does an IAM role provide?", Options: [4]string{"Temporary credentials", "Permanent access keys", "Root passwords", "SSH keys"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "An IAM role is a set of permissions that can be assumed by AWS services or users. Provides temporary credentials (STS). No long-term credentials. EC2 instances, Lambda, other services assume roles. More secure than access keys. Essential for service-to-service access. Enables least privilege access."},
		{ID: 57, Text: "What can IAM policies be attached to?", Options: [4]string{"Users, groups, or roles", "Only EC2 instances", "Only S3 buckets", "Only Lambda functions"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "An IAM policy is a JSON document defining permissions. Specifies what actions are allowed/denied on which resources. Attached to users, groups, or roles. Can be managed (AWS-provided) or custom. Effect: Allow or Deny. Essential for access control. Principle of least privilege should be applied."},
		{ID: 58, Text: "What does the least privilege principle recommend?", Options: [4]string{"Grant minimum permissions needed", "Grant all permissions by default", "Use root account for tasks", "Share credentials freely"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Least privilege is a security principle granting minimum permissions needed. Users/services get only what they need, nothing more. Reduces attack surface. Essential security best practice. Review and remove unused permissions regularly. Prevents privilege escalation and reduces risk."},
		{ID: 59, Text: "What additional authentication does MFA require beyond a password?", Options: [4]string{"A device token or code", "A second password", "A security question", "An email confirmation"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication) requires additional authentication beyond password. Something you know (password) + something you have (device). Virtual MFA devices (apps) or hardware MFA devices. Required for root account, recommended for IAM users. Essential for account security. Prevents unauthorized access even if password is compromised."},
		{ID: 60, Text: "Where is data encrypted at rest stored?", Options: [4]string{"On disk", "In transit", "In memory", "In cache"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Encryption at rest encrypts data stored on disk. Protects data when not in use. S3, EBS, RDS support encryption. Uses KMS keys or service-managed keys. Required for compliance (HIPAA, PCI-DSS). Essential for data security. Prevents unauthorized access to stored data."},
		{ID: 61, Text: "What protocol is commonly used for encryption in transit?", Options: [4]string{"TLS/SSL", "FTP", "Telnet", "HTTP"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Encryption in transit encrypts data during transmission. Uses TLS/SSL protocols. Protects data between client and server, between services. Required for compliance. HTTPS for web traffic, encrypted connections for databases. Essential for data security. Prevents interception and man-in-the-middle attacks."},
		{ID: 62, Text: "Are EBS snapshots incremental or full backups?", Options: [4]string{"Incremental", "Full", "Differential", "Synthetic"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of an EBS volume. Stored in S3. Incremental - only changed blocks saved. Can create volumes from snapshots. Used for backups, disaster recovery, volume migration. Can be automated with lifecycle policies. Essential for data protection."},
		{ID: 63, Text: "What does AMI stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Amazon Machine Image", "Amazon Memory Instance", "Automated Management Interface", "Advanced Monitoring Integration"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AMI (Amazon Machine Image) is a template for EC2 instances. Contains OS, application server, applications. Launch instances from AMIs. Can create custom AMIs from running instances. Shared AMIs available or create your own. Essential for consistent deployments. Enables golden images and fast scaling."},
		{ID: 64, Text: "What does an EC2 instance type determine?", Options: [4]string{"CPU, memory, and network performance", "Only storage capacity", "Only geographic location", "Only pricing model"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Instance type determines EC2 instance size and capabilities. Defines vCPU, memory, network performance, storage. Families: general purpose (t3, m5), compute optimized (c5), memory optimized (r5), storage optimized (i3). Choose based on workload requirements. Right-sizing saves costs. Essential for performance optimization."},
		{ID: 65, Text: "What is the main risk of using Spot instances?", Options: [4]string{"Can be interrupted with 2-minute notice", "More expensive than On-Demand", "Cannot use EBS volumes", "Require 1-year commitment"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Spot instances use spare EC2 capacity at up to 90% discount. Can be interrupted with 2-minute notice when capacity needed. Ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads. Set maximum price. Cost-effective for batch processing, data analysis, CI/CD. Not suitable for persistent workloads. Essential for cost optimization."},
		{ID: 66, Text: "What commitment terms are available for Reserved Instances?", Options: [4]string{"1 or 3 years", "1 or 5 years", "Monthly", "Weekly"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Reserved Instances provide discount (up to 75%) for 1-3 year commitment. Predictable workloads benefit most. Payment options: All Upfront, Partial Upfront, No Upfront. Can be Standard (modifiable) or Convertible (exchangeable). Regional or Zonal. Essential for cost optimization of steady-state workloads."},
		{ID: 67, Text: "What is the billing commitment for On-Demand instances?", Options: [4]string{"No commitment", "1 year minimum", "3 months minimum", "Pay upfront"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "On-demand instances are pay-as-you-go with no upfront costs or commitments. Most flexible pricing. Pay for compute capacity by the hour/second. No long-term commitments. Good for unpredictable workloads, testing, short-term projects. Most expensive but most flexible. Default pricing model."},
		{ID: 68, Text: "Is an S3 bucket name globally unique?", Options: [4]string{"Yes, across all AWS accounts", "No, only within your account", "Only within a region", "Only within an AZ"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A bucket is an S3 container for objects. Globally unique name across all AWS accounts. Region-specific. Can store unlimited objects. Configure versioning, lifecycle, encryption, access policies per bucket. Essential S3 concept. Organizes objects. Root of S3 storage hierarchy."},
		{ID: 69, Text: "What does S3 versioning protect against?", Options: [4]string{"Accidental deletion and overwrite", "Unauthorized access", "DDoS attacks", "High latency"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "S3 versioning keeps multiple versions of objects. Protects against accidental deletion/overwrite. Can restore previous versions. Each version has unique version ID. Can enable MFA Delete for extra protection. Increases storage costs. Essential for data protection and compliance. Enables point-in-time recovery."},
		{ID: 70, Text: "What can S3 lifecycle policies automatically do?", Options: [4]string{"Transition objects between storage classes", "Encrypt objects", "Create buckets", "Generate access keys"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects between storage classes or delete them. Move to cheaper storage (Standard → IA → Glacier). Delete old objects. Based on age or date. Reduces storage costs automatically. Essential for cost optimization. Set at bucket level. Can have multiple rules."},
		{ID: 71, Text: "What must be enabled on the source bucket for S3 replication?", Options: [4]string{"Versioning", "Encryption", "Logging", "Static website hosting"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "S3 replication copies objects to another bucket (cross-region or same-region). Asynchronous replication. Use cases: disaster recovery, compliance, latency reduction, backup. Can replicate to different storage classes. Versioning must be enabled on source. Essential for data redundancy and compliance."},
		{ID: 72, Text: "What do VPC endpoints enable?", Options: [4]string{"Private connectivity to AWS services", "Public internet access", "Cross-region VPC peering", "Direct Connect setup"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "VPC endpoints provide private connectivity to AWS services. No internet gateway, NAT, or VPN needed. Two types: Gateway endpoints (S3, DynamoDB) and Interface endpoints (other services). Reduces data transfer costs. Improves security. Essential for private access to AWS services. Keeps traffic within AWS network."},
		{ID: 73, Text: "What traffic direction does NAT Gateway support?", Options: [4]string{"Outbound only from private subnets", "Inbound only to private subnets", "Both inbound and outbound", "No internet traffic"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "NAT Gateway allows instances in private subnets to access internet outbound. Managed service (no EC2 instance to manage). Highly available (deploy in multiple AZs). Provides static IP addresses. Required for private subnet internet access (updates, downloads). Essential for private subnet connectivity. More reliable than NAT instance."},
		{ID: 74, Text: "How many Internet Gateways can be attached to a VPC?", Options: [4]string{"One", "Two", "Five", "Unlimited"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Internet Gateway enables VPC to connect to internet. One per VPC. Provides public IP addresses. Required for public subnets. Two-way communication (inbound and outbound). Attached to VPC, added to route tables. Essential for public internet access. Enables public-facing resources."},
		{ID: 75, Text: "How many Availability Zones can a subnet span?", Options: [4]string{"One", "Two", "Three", "All AZs in a region"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A subnet is a range of IP addresses in a VPC. Subdivides VPC into smaller networks. Each subnet is in one AZ. Can be public (internet gateway route) or private (NAT gateway). Route table controls traffic. Essential VPC component. Enables network segmentation and security."},
		{ID: 76, Text: "What makes a subnet public?", Options: [4]string{"Route to internet gateway", "Public IP addresses only", "Security groups", "Being in us-east-1"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A public subnet has a route to an internet gateway. Instances can have public IPs. Accessible from internet (if security groups allow). Used for load balancers, NAT gateways, bastion hosts. Route table includes 0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway. Essential for public-facing resources."},
		{ID: 77, Text: "What does a private subnet lack that a public subnet has?", Options: [4]string{"Direct route to internet gateway", "Security groups", "IP addresses", "Route tables"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A private subnet has no direct route to internet gateway. Instances typically have private IPs only. Can use NAT Gateway for outbound internet. More secure - not directly accessible from internet. Used for databases, application servers. Route table has no internet gateway route. Essential for backend resources."},
		{ID: 78, Text: "What do route tables contain?", Options: [4]string{"Routing rules for network traffic", "Security policies", "IAM permissions", "CloudWatch logs"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Route tables contain rules (routes) that determine where network traffic is directed. Each subnet must be associated with a route table. Routes specify destination (CIDR) and target (gateway, instance, etc.). Default route table created with VPC. Custom route tables for different subnets. Essential for network routing."},
		{ID: 79, Text: "What formats can CloudFormation templates be written in?", Options: [4]string{"YAML or JSON", "Only JSON", "Only XML", "Python or JavaScript"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A CloudFormation template is a YAML or JSON file defining AWS resources. Declarative - describes desired state. Contains Resources, Parameters, Outputs, Mappings, Conditions. Version controlled. Reusable and shareable. Essential for Infrastructure as Code. Single source of truth for infrastructure."},
		{ID: 80, Text: "What are CloudFormation parameters used for?", Options: [4]string{"Template input values", "Output values", "Resource types", "Stack names"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Parameters are inputs to CloudFormation templates. Make templates reusable with different values. Types: String, Number, List, CommaDelimitedList. Can have defaults, allowed values, descriptions. Provided at stack creation/update. Essential for template flexibility. Enables one template for multiple environments."},
		{ID: 81, Text: "What can CloudFormation outputs be used for?", Options: [4]string{"Exporting values for other stacks", "Defining resources", "Setting parameters", "Creating templates"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Outputs export values from a CloudFormation stack. Can reference outputs from other stacks. Useful for sharing information (IPs, ARNs, etc.). Displayed after stack creation. Can be exported for cross-stack references. Essential for stack integration. Provides stack information to users/other stacks."},
		{ID: 82, Text: "What section of a CloudFormation template defines AWS components?", Options: [4]string{"Resources", "Parameters", "Outputs", "Mappings"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Resources are AWS components defined in CloudFormation templates. Each resource has a Type (e.g., AWS::EC2::Instance) and Properties. CloudFormation provisions actual AWS resources from template. Core template element. Can reference other resources. Essential for infrastructure definition. Represents actual AWS resources."},
		{ID: 83, Text: "What does CloudFormation drift detection identify?", Options: [4]string{"Differences between template and actual configuration", "Cost overruns", "Security vulnerabilities", "Performance issues"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Drift detection identifies differences between template and actual resource configuration. Detects manual changes outside CloudFormation. Helps maintain infrastructure consistency. Can detect configuration drift. Essential for compliance and change management. Ensures infrastructure matches template definition."},
		{ID: 84, Text: "What does a CloudFormation change set allow you to do?", Options: [4]string{"Preview changes before applying", "Revert to previous version", "Delete all resources", "Create new accounts"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "A change set previews modifications before applying stack update. Shows what will change, be created, deleted, replaced. Review before executing. Can create multiple change sets. Execute the one you want. Essential for safe updates. Prevents unexpected changes."},
		{ID: 85, Text: "What can trigger a CloudFormation stack update?", Options: [4]string{"Applying a new template", "Viewing stack events", "Reading outputs", "Creating change sets"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Stack update modifies an existing stack with a new template. Can add, modify, or delete resources. CloudFormation determines changes needed. Some changes require replacement (new resource). Use change sets to preview. Essential for infrastructure evolution. Maintains stack state and relationships."},
		{ID: 86, Text: "What happens to resources when a stack is deleted?", Options: [4]string{"Deleted in reverse dependency order", "All deleted simultaneously", "Resources are retained automatically", "Nothing happens"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Stack deletion removes a CloudFormation stack and its resources. Deletes resources in reverse dependency order. Can retain resources with DeletionPolicy. Useful for cleanup and cost management. Removes all stack-managed resources. Essential for infrastructure lifecycle management. Use carefully - can delete production resources."},
		{ID: 87, Text: "Where are nested stacks created?", Options: [4]string{"Within another stack", "In separate accounts", "Only in us-east-1", "Outside CloudFormation"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Nested stacks are stacks created within another stack. Enables modular templates. Reuse common components. Simplify complex templates. Parent stack manages nested stacks. Essential for template organization. Promotes reusability and maintainability."},
		{ID: 88, Text: "How do cross-stack references share data?", Options: [4]string{"Export outputs and import in another stack", "Direct resource access", "S3 buckets", "Environment variables"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Cross-stack references allow stacks to share values. Export outputs from one stack, import in another. Enables stack dependencies. Loose coupling between stacks. Essential for modular architectures. Allows independent stack management while sharing data."},
		{ID: 89, Text: "What does SAM stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Serverless Application Model", "Simple Access Management", "System Administration Manager", "Security Audit Module"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "SAM (Serverless Application Model) is an extension of CloudFormation for serverless applications. Simplified syntax for Lambda, API Gateway, DynamoDB. Transforms SAM templates to CloudFormation. Easier than raw CloudFormation for serverless. Essential for serverless development. Reduces boilerplate for Lambda functions."},
		{ID: 90, Text: "What does AWS CDK generate?", Options: [4]string{"CloudFormation templates", "Docker images", "AMIs", "Lambda functions"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CDK (Cloud Development Kit) defines infrastructure using programming languages (TypeScript, Python, Java, C#, Go). Generates CloudFormation templates. Object-oriented approach. Reusable constructs. Type-safe infrastructure. Essential for developers preferring code over YAML/JSON. Combines power of CloudFormation with programming languages."},
		{ID: 91, Text: "How many pillars does the AWS Well-Architected Framework have?", Options: [4]string{"Five", "Three", "Seven", "Ten"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Well-Architected Framework provides architectural best practices. Five pillars: Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization, Operational Excellence. Helps design and operate secure, high-performing systems. Review architecture against framework. Essential for building production-ready systems. AWS best practices guide."},
		{ID: 92, Text: "Which is NOT a pillar of the Well-Architected Framework?", Options: [4]string{"Scalability", "Security", "Reliability", "Cost Optimization"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "The 5 pillars are: Security (protect systems/data), Reliability (recover from failures), Performance Efficiency (use resources efficiently), Cost Optimization (avoid unnecessary costs), Operational Excellence (run/monitor systems). Foundation of Well-Architected Framework. Essential for building robust architectures. Balance all pillars."},
		{ID: 93, Text: "Which pillar focuses on avoiding unnecessary costs?", Options: [4]string{"Cost Optimization", "Performance Efficiency", "Operational Excellence", "Security"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Cost optimization pillar focuses on avoiding unnecessary costs. Right-size resources, use Reserved Instances, eliminate unused resources, optimize data transfer. Monitor costs with Cost Explorer. Use Trusted Advisor recommendations. Essential for efficient cloud spending. Maximize value while minimizing costs."},
		{ID: 94, Text: "Which pillar emphasizes automating changes and monitoring systems?", Options: [4]string{"Operational Excellence", "Security", "Reliability", "Performance Efficiency"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Operational Excellence pillar focuses on running and monitoring systems. Automate changes, respond to events, define standards. Use CloudFormation, CodePipeline, CloudWatch. Document procedures. Essential for efficient operations. Enables continuous improvement and learning from failures."},
		{ID: 95, Text: "Which pillar focuses on using computing resources efficiently?", Options: [4]string{"Performance Efficiency", "Cost Optimization", "Security", "Operational Excellence"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Performance Efficiency pillar focuses on using computing resources efficiently. Right-size resources, use advanced technologies, monitor performance, trade-offs. Auto Scaling, caching, CDN. Essential for optimal performance. Maximize performance while minimizing cost. Adapt to changing requirements."},
		{ID: 96, Text: "Which pillar focuses on system recovery from failures?", Options: [4]string{"Reliability", "Security", "Performance Efficiency", "Cost Optimization"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Reliability pillar focuses on system recovery from failures. Design for failure, test recovery procedures, scale horizontally, automate recovery. Multi-AZ, Auto Scaling, health checks. Essential for production systems. Ensures system can recover from infrastructure/service failures automatically."},
		{ID: 97, Text: "Which pillar emphasizes protecting systems and data?", Options: [4]string{"Security", "Reliability", "Operational Excellence", "Cost Optimization"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Security pillar focuses on protecting systems and data. Implement strong identity foundation (IAM), enable traceability (CloudTrail), apply security at all layers, automate security best practices, protect data in transit and at rest. Essential for secure architectures. Defense in depth approach."},
		{ID: 98, Text: "What does Trusted Advisor provide?", Options: [4]string{"Best practice recommendations", "Database backups", "Code compilation", "Container orchestration"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Trusted Advisor analyzes AWS account and provides recommendations. Checks: cost optimization, security, fault tolerance, performance, service limits. Free tier: core checks. Business/Enterprise: all checks. Essential for optimization. Helps identify opportunities to save money and improve security/reliability."},
		{ID: 99, Text: "What can Cost Explorer visualize?", Options: [4]string{"AWS costs and spending trends", "Network topology", "Application logs", "Database schemas"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Cost Explorer visualizes and analyzes AWS costs. View spending by service, account, tag, etc. Historical data and forecasts. Identify cost drivers. Create custom reports. Essential for cost management. Helps understand where money is spent. Enables data-driven cost optimization decisions."},
		{ID: 100, Text: "What does AWS Organizations primarily manage?", Options: [4]string{"Multiple AWS accounts", "EC2 instances", "S3 buckets", "Lambda functions"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts. Consolidated billing - single bill for all accounts. Organizational units (OUs) for grouping. Service Control Policies (SCPs) for account governance. Centralized management. Essential for enterprises. Simplifies account management and cost allocation. Enables policy-based account management."},
		{ID: 101, Text: "What can SCPs do in AWS Organizations?", Options: [4]string{"Control what services can be used in accounts", "Grant permissions directly", "Create IAM users", "Launch EC2 instances"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "SCPs are organization-wide policies that control what services and actions can be used in member accounts. Act as guardrails - cannot grant permissions, only deny. Applied at OU or account level. Cannot restrict root user. Essential for centralized governance. Helps enforce compliance and security policies across organization."},
		{ID: 102, Text: "What does Control Tower help set up?", Options: [4]string{"Multi-account landing zone", "Single EC2 instance", "Database cluster", "CDN distribution"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Control Tower automates setup of secure, compliant multi-account AWS environment. Creates landing zone with best practices. Manages accounts, guardrails, compliance. Built on Organizations, Config, CloudTrail. Essential for enterprises starting AWS journey. Provides prescriptive guidance and automated setup. Simplifies multi-account governance."},
		{ID: 103, Text: "What does AWS Backup provide?", Options: [4]string{"Centralized backup across AWS services", "Only S3 backups", "Only EC2 backups", "Email service"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AWS Backup provides centralized backup service across AWS services. Supports EC2, EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS, Storage Gateway, and more. Policy-based backup scheduling. Cross-region backup. Lifecycle management. Essential for unified backup strategy. Simplifies backup management across multiple services."},
		{ID: 104, Text: "What does DataSync transfer?", Options: [4]string{"Data between on-premises and AWS", "Only emails", "Only logs", "DNS records"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "DataSync transfers data between on-premises and AWS storage services. Faster than open-source tools. Uses parallel streams and compression. Incremental transfers. Supports S3, EFS, FSx. Essential for data migration and hybrid cloud. Reduces transfer time and costs compared to standard tools."},
		{ID: 105, Text: "How many types of Storage Gateway are there?", Options: [4]string{"Three (File, Volume, Tape)", "One", "Five", "Ten"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS storage. Three types: File Gateway (S3 via NFS/SMB), Volume Gateway (EBS via iSCSI), Tape Gateway (S3 Glacier via VTL). Caches frequently accessed data locally. Essential for hybrid cloud storage. Enables seamless integration between on-premises and cloud."},
		{ID: 106, Text: "What type of API does AppSync build?", Options: [4]string{"GraphQL", "REST only", "SOAP", "gRPC"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AppSync is a managed GraphQL service. Builds scalable APIs with real-time subscriptions. Integrates with DynamoDB, Lambda, Elasticsearch, HTTP APIs. Handles offline support, conflict resolution. Pay per query. Essential for mobile and web applications. Simplifies building GraphQL APIs with real-time capabilities."},
		{ID: 107, Text: "What types of applications does Amplify target?", Options: [4]string{"Mobile and web applications", "Only mainframe apps", "Only batch jobs", "Only databases"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Amplify is a full-stack platform for building mobile and web applications. Provides authentication, storage, API, hosting, CI/CD. Integrates with Cognito, AppSync, S3, CloudFront. Simplifies frontend development. Essential for rapid application development. Handles backend infrastructure automatically."},
		{ID: 108, Text: "What proxy technology does App Mesh use?", Options: [4]string{"Envoy", "Nginx", "HAProxy", "Apache"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "App Mesh is a service mesh for microservices. Manages service-to-service communication. Provides traffic management, observability, security. Works with ECS, EKS, EC2, Fargate. Envoy proxy-based. Essential for complex microservices architectures. Simplifies service communication and provides consistent networking."},
		{ID: 109, Text: "What does Global Accelerator provide?", Options: [4]string{"Static IP addresses for global routing", "Dynamic IPs only", "Database endpoints", "SSH access"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Global Accelerator improves availability and performance of applications. Uses AWS edge locations to route traffic. Provides static IP addresses. Automatic failover. Routes to nearest healthy endpoint. Essential for global applications. Reduces latency and improves availability compared to single-region deployments."},
		{ID: 110, Text: "Where does AWS Outposts deploy infrastructure?", Options: [4]string{"On-premises data centers", "Only in AWS regions", "Only edge locations", "In customer vehicles"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Outposts brings AWS infrastructure to on-premises data centers. Same APIs, services, tools as AWS cloud. For low-latency requirements, data residency, local data processing. Fully managed by AWS. Essential for hybrid cloud with strict latency or data residency requirements. Seamless extension of AWS cloud."},
		{ID: 111, Text: "Where does AWS Wavelength deploy infrastructure?", Options: [4]string{"Inside 5G networks", "In data centers", "In cloud regions", "On satellites"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Wavelength embeds AWS compute and storage in 5G networks. Ultra-low latency for mobile applications. Deploy at carrier edge locations. Essential for real-time mobile applications (gaming, AR/VR, ML inference). Reduces latency to single-digit milliseconds. Extends AWS to edge of 5G networks."},
		{ID: 112, Text: "What latency does AWS Local Zones provide?", Options: [4]string{"Single-digit milliseconds", "Hundreds of milliseconds", "Multiple seconds", "Multiple minutes"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Local Zones are AWS infrastructure in metropolitan areas. Brings AWS services closer to end users. Single-digit millisecond latency. Supports EC2, EBS, VPC, ECS, EKS. Essential for latency-sensitive applications. Extends AWS regions to major cities. Reduces latency for local users."},
		{ID: 113, Text: "What does Fargate eliminate?", Options: [4]string{"Server management", "Load balancing", "Data storage", "IAM roles"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Fargate is serverless compute for containers. Run containers without managing servers. Works with ECS and EKS. Pay for vCPU and memory used. No EC2 instances to manage. Essential for container workloads without infrastructure management. Simplifies container deployment and scaling."},
		{ID: 114, Text: "What type of computing workloads does AWS Batch handle?", Options: [4]string{"Batch computing", "Real-time streaming", "Interactive queries", "Web hosting"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Batch runs large-scale batch computing workloads. Dynamically provisions compute resources. Handles job scheduling, queuing, execution. Supports EC2 and Spot Instances. Essential for HPC, data processing, ML training. Pay only for resources used. Automatically scales based on workload."},
		{ID: 115, Text: "What service model does Elastic Beanstalk provide?", Options: [4]string{"Platform as a Service", "Infrastructure as a Service", "Software as a Service", "Backend as a Service"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Elastic Beanstalk is a PaaS for deploying applications. Supports multiple languages (Java, .NET, PHP, Node.js, Python, Ruby, Go, Docker). Handles capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto-scaling, monitoring. Developer focuses on code. Essential for rapid deployment. Simplifies application deployment and management."},
		{ID: 116, Text: "What does Amazon Lightsail provide?", Options: [4]string{"Simple VPS with fixed pricing", "Kubernetes clusters", "Data warehouses", "IoT devices"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Lightsail provides virtual private servers with fixed pricing. Simplified EC2 alternative. Includes compute, storage, networking. Pre-configured application stacks. Predictable monthly pricing. Essential for simple applications, websites, development. Easier than EC2 for basic use cases. Good for getting started with AWS."},
		{ID: 117, Text: "What does App Runner deploy from?", Options: [4]string{"Source code or container image", "Only source code", "Only pre-built binaries", "Only VM images"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "App Runner builds and runs containerized applications from source code or container image. Automatic scaling, load balancing, encryption. Pay for compute used. Essential for web applications and APIs. Simplifies container deployment. No infrastructure management required."},
		{ID: 118, Text: "What does AWS Proton manage?", Options: [4]string{"Container and serverless application infrastructure", "Databases only", "S3 buckets", "IAM users"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Proton provides self-service container and serverless application deployment. Platform teams define templates, developers deploy. Standardizes infrastructure. Integrates with CodePipeline, CodeBuild. Essential for platform engineering. Enables self-service while maintaining standards. Reduces operational overhead."},
		{ID: 119, Text: "What version control system does CodeCommit use?", Options: [4]string{"Git", "SVN", "Mercurial", "CVS"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CodeCommit is a managed Git repository service. Private, scalable, secure. Integrates with CodePipeline, CodeBuild. No size limits. Pay for storage and requests. Essential for AWS-native CI/CD. Fully managed alternative to GitHub/GitLab. Encrypted at rest and in transit."},
		{ID: 120, Text: "What does CodeArtifact store?", Options: [4]string{"Software packages and dependencies", "Source code", "Docker images only", "Database backups"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CodeArtifact is a managed artifact repository. Supports Maven, npm, pip, NuGet, and more. Private package storage. Integrates with CodeBuild, CodePipeline. Pay for storage and requests. Essential for secure dependency management. Reduces external dependency on public repositories."},
		{ID: 121, Text: "What does CodeStar provide?", Options: [4]string{"Unified project dashboard and CI/CD templates", "Only code editing", "Only testing", "Only monitoring"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CodeStar provides unified project management for software development. Pre-configured project templates. Sets up CI/CD pipelines automatically. Dashboard for project activity. Integrates CodeCommit, CodeBuild, CodeDeploy, CodePipeline. Essential for quick project setup. Simplifies getting started with AWS development tools."},
		{ID: 122, Text: "What does Device Farm test?", Options: [4]string{"Mobile and web applications", "Only server applications", "Only databases", "Only networks"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Device Farm tests mobile apps on real devices in the cloud. Supports iOS, Android, web apps. Automated testing or manual testing. Access to hundreds of device configurations. Essential for mobile app quality assurance. Reduces need for physical device labs. Pay per device minute used."},
		{ID: 123, Text: "Where does Cloud9 run?", Options: [4]string{"In the browser", "On local machine only", "On mobile devices", "On IoT devices"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Cloud9 is a cloud-based integrated development environment. Browser-based, no installation needed. Pre-configured with AWS SDKs. Collaborative coding. Connects to EC2 or own servers. Essential for cloud-native development. Simplifies development environment setup. Integrated with AWS services."},
		{ID: 124, Text: "What does Session Manager enable?", Options: [4]string{"Browser-based shell access to EC2", "Database queries", "S3 uploads", "Load balancing"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Session Manager provides secure shell access to EC2 instances. No SSH keys or bastion hosts needed. Browser-based or CLI. Encrypted sessions. Audit logs in CloudTrail. Essential for secure instance access. Eliminates need for SSH key management. Integrated with IAM for access control."},
		{ID: 125, Text: "What does Parameter Store store?", Options: [4]string{"Configuration data and secrets", "Only source code", "Only Docker images", "Only database backups"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Parameter Store stores configuration data and secrets. Hierarchical organization. Supports plaintext and encrypted (KMS) values. Versioning and history. Free for standard parameters. Essential for application configuration. Better than hardcoding values. Integrates with Lambda, ECS, EC2."},
		{ID: 126, Text: "What does Patch Manager automate?", Options: [4]string{"Patching of EC2 and on-premises servers", "Code deployments", "S3 uploads", "DNS updates"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Patch Manager automates patching of EC2 instances and on-premises servers. Schedules patching, maintains patch compliance. Supports Windows and Linux. Can use maintenance windows. Essential for security compliance. Reduces manual patching effort. Helps maintain system security."},
		{ID: 127, Text: "Where does Run Command execute commands?", Options: [4]string{"Remotely on EC2 instances", "Only locally", "Only on S3", "Only on Lambda"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Run Command executes commands on EC2 instances remotely. No SSH needed. Can target multiple instances. Secure, auditable. Integrates with IAM. Essential for bulk operations. Useful for configuration management, software installation, troubleshooting. Logs all commands in CloudTrail."},
		{ID: 128, Text: "What does Systems Manager Automation execute?", Options: [4]string{"Operational tasks and runbooks", "Only user authentication", "Only file transfers", "Only backups"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Automation executes operational tasks and runbooks. Automate common tasks (instance refresh, AMI creation, etc.). Can be triggered manually or by events. Self-healing capabilities. Essential for operational automation. Reduces manual intervention. Enables consistent, repeatable operations."},
		{ID: 129, Text: "What configuration tools does OpsWorks support?", Options: [4]string{"Chef and Puppet", "Only Ansible", "Only Terraform", "Only CloudFormation"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "OpsWorks is a configuration management service using Chef and Puppet. Manages application stacks and layers. Handles deployment, scaling, monitoring. Supports EC2, on-premises, hybrid. Essential for Chef/Puppet users. Provides managed Chef/Puppet servers. Simplifies configuration management at scale."},
		{ID: 130, Text: "What does Service Catalog provide?", Options: [4]string{"Catalog of approved IT services", "Only DNS services", "Only backup services", "Only monitoring services"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Service Catalog provides catalog of approved IT services. Self-service provisioning. Enforces governance and compliance. Pre-approved products (CloudFormation templates). Essential for IT governance. Enables self-service while maintaining control. Reduces shadow IT and ensures compliance."},
		{ID: 131, Text: "What does AWS Marketplace provide?", Options: [4]string{"Digital catalog of third-party software", "Only AWS services", "Only training materials", "Only support tickets"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Marketplace is a digital catalog of software from third-party vendors. AMIs, containers, SaaS products. One-click deployment. Pay through AWS bill. Essential for finding and deploying software. Simplifies software procurement. Integrated with AWS services."},
		{ID: 132, Text: "What is AWS QuickSight used for?", Options: [4]string{"Business intelligence", "Database backups", "Code compilation", "Container orchestration"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "QuickSight is a cloud-based business intelligence service. Creates interactive dashboards and visualizations. Connects to AWS data sources (S3, RDS, Redshift, etc.). Pay per user per month. Essential for data visualization. Self-service BI for business users. No infrastructure to manage."},
		{ID: 133, Text: "What does Data Pipeline orchestrate?", Options: [4]string{"Data workflows", "Network routing", "User authentication", "Email delivery"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Data Pipeline orchestrates data workflows. Moves and transforms data between AWS services and on-premises. Scheduled execution. Handles dependencies, retries, failures. Essential for data ETL workflows. Managed alternative to running ETL on EC2. Pay per pipeline execution."},
		{ID: 134, Text: "What does Lake Formation simplify?", Options: [4]string{"Building and managing data lakes", "Building mobile apps", "Managing DNS", "Sending emails"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Lake Formation simplifies building and managing data lakes. Centralized permissions and security. Automates ETL from databases. Works with S3, Glue, Athena, Redshift. Essential for data lake governance. Simplifies data lake setup and management. Provides fine-grained access control."},
		{ID: 135, Text: "What is AWS Data Exchange?", Options: [4]string{"Data marketplace", "Code repository", "Container registry", "DNS service"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Data Exchange is a data marketplace. Find, subscribe to, and use third-party data. Data products from various providers. Pay per subscription. Essential for accessing external data. Simplifies data acquisition. Integrated with AWS analytics services."},
		{ID: 136, Text: "What type of platform is SageMaker?", Options: [4]string{"Machine learning", "Web hosting", "Email service", "DNS management"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "SageMaker is a fully managed machine learning platform. Build, train, deploy ML models. Integrated Jupyter notebooks. Built-in algorithms. AutoML capabilities. Essential for ML workloads. Simplifies ML development and deployment. Handles infrastructure management."},
		{ID: 137, Text: "What type of processing does Comprehend provide?", Options: [4]string{"Natural language processing", "Image processing", "Video processing", "Audio processing"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Comprehend is a natural language processing service. Extracts insights from text. Sentiment analysis, entity recognition, language detection, key phrases. Uses machine learning. Essential for text analysis. No ML expertise required. Pay per document or character analyzed."},
		{ID: 138, Text: "What does Rekognition analyze?", Options: [4]string{"Images and videos", "Only text", "Only audio", "Only databases"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Rekognition analyzes images and videos using machine learning. Face detection/recognition, object detection, text detection, content moderation. API-based service. Essential for image/video analysis. No ML expertise required. Pay per image/video analyzed or per minute of video."},
		{ID: 139, Text: "What does Polly convert?", Options: [4]string{"Text to speech", "Speech to text", "Image to text", "Video to audio"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Polly converts text to lifelike speech. Multiple voices and languages. Neural voices for natural sound. SSML support for control. Essential for voice applications. Pay per character. Integrates with other AWS services. Useful for accessibility and voice interfaces."},
		{ID: 140, Text: "What does Transcribe convert?", Options: [4]string{"Speech to text", "Text to speech", "Image to text", "Video to audio"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Transcribe converts speech to text. Supports multiple languages and audio formats. Real-time and batch transcription. Custom vocabulary support. Essential for audio transcription. Pay per second of audio. Useful for meeting transcripts, captions, content indexing."},
		{ID: 141, Text: "What does Translate provide?", Options: [4]string{"Language translation", "Speech recognition", "Image recognition", "Video encoding"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Translate translates text between languages. Supports many language pairs. Neural machine translation. Real-time and batch. Custom terminology support. Essential for multilingual applications. Pay per character. Useful for content localization and translation."},
		{ID: 142, Text: "What does Lex build?", Options: [4]string{"Chatbots", "Databases", "Web servers", "File storage"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Lex builds conversational interfaces (chatbots). Voice and text. Natural language understanding. Integrates with Lambda, Connect, mobile apps. Powers Alexa. Essential for building chatbots. Pay per request. Simplifies conversational AI development."},
		{ID: 143, Text: "What is AWS Connect?", Options: [4]string{"Contact center", "Video streaming", "File storage", "Database service"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Connect is a cloud-based contact center service. Omnichannel (voice, chat, email). Pay per minute of use. Integrates with Lex, Lambda, CRM systems. Essential for customer service. No infrastructure to manage. Scales automatically. Reduces contact center costs."},
		{ID: 144, Text: "What is AWS Chime?", Options: [4]string{"Video conferencing", "Database service", "File storage", "Load balancer"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Chime is a communications service for online meetings. Video conferencing, screen sharing, chat. SDK for embedding in applications. Pay per user or per meeting. Essential for video communications. High-quality audio/video. Integrates with AWS services."},
		{ID: 145, Text: "What does WorkSpaces provide?", Options: [4]string{"Virtual desktops", "Physical servers", "Network switches", "Storage arrays"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "WorkSpaces provides managed virtual desktops (DaaS). Windows or Linux. Access from anywhere. Pay monthly or hourly. Essential for remote work. Simplifies desktop management. No infrastructure to manage. Secure and scalable."},
		{ID: 146, Text: "What is AWS WorkDocs?", Options: [4]string{"Document storage and sharing", "Video streaming", "Load balancing", "DNS service"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "WorkDocs is a secure document storage and sharing service. File versioning, comments, collaboration. Mobile apps. Integrates with Active Directory. Essential for document management. Pay per user per month. Alternative to SharePoint or Dropbox."},
		{ID: 147, Text: "What does WorkLink provide?", Options: [4]string{"Secure mobile access to websites", "Desktop virtualization", "Database replication", "Container orchestration"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "WorkLink provides secure mobile access to internal websites. No VPN needed. Streams content to mobile devices. Essential for mobile workforce. Simplifies secure mobile access. Pay per active user per month. Reduces VPN complexity."},
		{ID: 148, Text: "What does AppStream provide?", Options: [4]string{"Application streaming", "Database backups", "DNS routing", "Email delivery"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "AppStream streams desktop applications to users. No installation needed. Runs on AWS infrastructure. Pay per streaming instance hour. Essential for application delivery. Useful for graphics-intensive applications. Supports Windows applications."},
		{ID: 149, Text: "What does Ground Station provide?", Options: [4]string{"Satellite communication", "Ground transportation", "Weather forecasting", "Earthquake monitoring"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Ground Station provides satellite communication services. Control satellites, ingest satellite data. Pay per antenna-minute. Essential for satellite operations. Reduces need to build ground stations. Global network of ground stations."},
		{ID: 150, Text: "What does RoboMaker support?", Options: [4]string{"ROS (Robot Operating System)", "Only Windows apps", "Only mobile apps", "Only web apps"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "RoboMaker is a cloud-based robotics development platform. Simulate robots, run ROS applications. Extends ROS with cloud services. Essential for robotics development. Pay for compute and storage used. Simplifies robot simulation and testing."},
		{ID: 151, Text: "What protocols does IoT Core support?", Options: [4]string{"MQTT and HTTP", "Only FTP", "Only SMTP", "Only SSH"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "IoT Core connects IoT devices to AWS. Supports MQTT, HTTP, WebSockets. Billions of devices. Device shadows for state. Rules engine for routing. Essential for IoT applications. Pay per message. Scales to millions of devices. Integrates with Lambda, DynamoDB, S3."},
		{ID: 152, Text: "What does IoT Device Management manage?", Options: [4]string{"IoT device fleets", "Only web servers", "Only databases", "Only DNS records"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "IoT Device Management manages IoT device fleets. Remote management, monitoring, updates. Device registry, jobs, thing groups. Essential for large IoT deployments. Simplifies device lifecycle management. Reduces operational overhead."},
		{ID: 153, Text: "What type of data does IoT Analytics process?", Options: [4]string{"IoT time series data", "Only images", "Only videos", "Only audio"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "IoT Analytics analyzes IoT data. Time series data processing. Data transformation, enrichment. Integrates with QuickSight. Essential for IoT insights. Pay per message analyzed. Simplifies IoT data analysis. Handles large volumes of IoT data."},
		{ID: 154, Text: "Where does IoT Greengrass run?", Options: [4]string{"On edge devices", "Only in AWS cloud", "Only on mobile phones", "Only on web browsers"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "IoT Greengrass extends AWS to edge devices. Local compute, messaging, data sync. Works offline. Lambda functions run locally. Essential for edge computing. Reduces latency and bandwidth. Enables offline operation."},
		{ID: 155, Text: "What does FreeRTOS provide?", Options: [4]string{"Operating system for microcontrollers", "Web hosting", "Database management", "Email services"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "FreeRTOS is an operating system for microcontrollers. Small, low-power devices. Connects to AWS IoT. Open source. Essential for microcontroller-based IoT devices. Reduces development time. Enables secure IoT connectivity."},
		{ID: 156, Text: "What does MediaConvert do?", Options: [4]string{"Transcodes video files", "Only converts images", "Only converts audio", "Only converts text"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MediaConvert transcodes video files. Converts between formats, resolutions, bitrates. On-demand processing. Essential for video processing. Pay per minute of video. Supports many formats. Used for video-on-demand workflows."},
		{ID: 157, Text: "What does MediaLive encode?", Options: [4]string{"Live video", "Only static images", "Only text", "Only audio"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MediaLive encodes live video for broadcast and streaming. Real-time encoding. Multiple output formats. DVR functionality. Essential for live streaming. Pay per channel hour. Used for live TV, events, streaming."},
		{ID: 158, Text: "What does MediaPackage prepare?", Options: [4]string{"Video for delivery", "Only emails", "Only databases", "Only web pages"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MediaPackage prepares and protects video for delivery. Packaging for different devices. DRM support. Time-shifted viewing. Essential for video delivery. Pay per GB of data processed. Works with MediaLive and MediaConvert."},
		{ID: 159, Text: "What is MediaStore optimized for?", Options: [4]string{"Media storage", "General file storage", "Database storage", "Email storage"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MediaStore is storage optimized for media. Low latency for live streaming. S3-compatible API. Essential for media workflows. Pay for storage and requests. Used with MediaLive for live streaming. Optimized for media access patterns."},
		{ID: 160, Text: "What does MediaTailor insert?", Options: [4]string{"Server-side ads into video", "Only subtitles", "Only watermarks", "Only thumbnails"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MediaTailor inserts server-side ads into video streams. Personalized ad insertion. Works with live and VOD. Essential for monetization. Pay per ad decision request. Increases ad revenue. Better user experience than client-side ads."},
		{ID: 161, Text: "What does Kinesis Video Streams ingest?", Options: [4]string{"Video streams", "Only text", "Only images", "Only audio"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Kinesis Video Streams ingests and stores video streams. Real-time video from devices. Integrates with Rekognition, SageMaker. Essential for video analytics. Pay per GB ingested and stored. Used for security cameras, live streaming, video analytics."},
		{ID: 162, Text: "What is MSK?", Options: [4]string{"Managed Apache Kafka", "Managed MongoDB", "Managed Redis", "Managed Cassandra"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MSK is a fully managed Apache Kafka service. Real-time streaming data platform. No infrastructure management. Auto-scaling. Essential for Kafka workloads. Pay for brokers and storage. Compatible with Kafka APIs. Simplifies Kafka operations."},
		{ID: 163, Text: "What is DocumentDB compatible with?", Options: [4]string{"MongoDB", "MySQL", "PostgreSQL", "Oracle"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "DocumentDB is a MongoDB-compatible document database. Fully managed. MongoDB API compatible. Multi-AZ, backups, monitoring. Essential for MongoDB workloads. Pay for instances and storage. Easier than self-managed MongoDB. High availability and durability."},
		{ID: 164, Text: "What type of database is Neptune?", Options: [4]string{"Graph database", "Relational database", "Key-value database", "Document database"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Neptune is a fully managed graph database. Optimized for relationship queries. Supports RDF and Property Graph models. Multi-AZ, backups. Essential for graph workloads. Pay for instances and storage. Used for social networks, recommendation engines, fraud detection."},
		{ID: 165, Text: "What type of database is Timestream?", Options: [4]string{"Time series database", "Graph database", "Document database", "Relational database"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Timestream is a serverless time series database. Optimized for IoT and DevOps data. Automatic scaling. Pay for storage and queries. Essential for time series data. Much faster and cheaper than relational databases for time series. Built-in data retention policies."},
		{ID: 166, Text: "What type of database is QLDB?", Options: [4]string{"Ledger database", "Graph database", "Document database", "Key-value database"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "QLDB (Quantum Ledger Database) is a ledger database. Immutable, cryptographically verifiable. Centralized (not blockchain). Append-only. Essential for audit trails, financial records. Pay for storage and I/O. Provides complete history of changes. Verifiable and tamper-proof."},
		{ID: 167, Text: "What engines does ElastiCache support?", Options: [4]string{"Redis and Memcached", "Only MongoDB", "Only Cassandra", "Only Neo4j"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "ElastiCache provides managed Redis and Memcached. In-memory caching. Reduces database load. Low latency. Essential for caching. Pay for nodes. Improves application performance. Supports clustering, replication, persistence."},
		{ID: 168, Text: "What is MemoryDB compatible with?", Options: [4]string{"Redis", "MongoDB", "Cassandra", "MySQL"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MemoryDB is a Redis-compatible durable in-memory database. Multi-AZ with automatic failover. Microsecond latency. Can be used as primary database. Essential for ultra-fast applications. Pay for nodes and storage. Combines speed of Redis with durability."},
		{ID: 169, Text: "What does DMS stand for?", Options: [4]string{"Database Migration Service", "Data Management System", "Document Management Service", "Deployment Management System"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "DMS (Database Migration Service) migrates databases to AWS. Homogeneous (same engine) or heterogeneous (different engine). One-time migration or continuous replication. Essential for database migration. Pay for compute hours. Reduces downtime. Supports many source and target databases."},
		{ID: 170, Text: "What does Schema Conversion Tool convert?", Options: [4]string{"Database schemas", "Only images", "Only videos", "Only text files"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Schema Conversion Tool converts database schemas. Heterogeneous migrations (e.g., Oracle to PostgreSQL). Converts schema and code. Essential for database engine migrations. Free tool. Reduces manual conversion effort. Identifies conversion issues."},
		{ID: 171, Text: "What does RDS Proxy provide?", Options: [4]string{"Connection pooling", "Backup services", "Monitoring only", "DNS routing"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "RDS Proxy is a database proxy service. Connection pooling for RDS. Reduces database connections. Serverless compatible (Lambda). Essential for serverless applications. Pay per vCPU hour. Improves scalability. Handles failover automatically."},
		{ID: 172, Text: "What does Aurora Serverless automatically do?", Options: [4]string{"Scales database capacity", "Encrypts data only", "Backs up data only", "Monitors logs only"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Aurora Serverless automatically scales database capacity. Scales to zero when not in use. Pay per ACU (Aurora Capacity Unit) per second. Essential for variable workloads. No capacity planning needed. Cost-effective for intermittent workloads."},
		{ID: 173, Text: "How many AWS regions can Aurora Global Database span?", Options: [4]string{"Multiple regions", "Only one region", "Only two regions", "Only three regions"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Aurora Global Database spans multiple AWS regions. Replication lag < 1 second. Read replicas in multiple regions. Essential for global applications. Reduces latency for global users. Disaster recovery across regions. Up to 16 read replicas."},
		{ID: 174, Text: "What does RDS automated backups enable?", Options: [4]string{"Point-in-time recovery", "Only full backups", "Only schema backups", "Only log backups"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "RDS automated backups enable point-in-time recovery. Backs up transaction logs. Retention period configurable (up to 35 days). Can copy to other regions. Essential for data protection. No additional cost (included). Enables recovery to any point in retention period."},
		{ID: 175, Text: "What are RDS Read Replicas used for?", Options: [4]string{"Read scaling", "Only backups", "Only monitoring", "Only logging"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Read Replicas are read-only copies of RDS database. Asynchronous replication. Offloads read traffic from primary. Up to 15 read replicas. Can promote to standalone. Essential for read scaling. Reduces load on primary database. Can be in different regions."},
		{ID: 176, Text: "What does RDS Multi-AZ provide?", Options: [4]string{"High availability", "Only read scaling", "Only backups", "Only monitoring"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Multi-AZ deploys RDS in multiple Availability Zones. Synchronous replication. Automatic failover (< 60 seconds). High availability. Essential for production. No code changes needed. Increases availability to 99.95%. Protects against AZ failures."},
		{ID: 177, Text: "What are RDS snapshots?", Options: [4]string{"Manual backups", "Automatic daily backups", "Continuous backups", "Log files"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "RDS snapshots are manual point-in-time backups. Stored in S3. Can copy to other regions. Can restore to new instance. Essential for backups and migrations. Pay for storage. Retained until deleted. Useful for disaster recovery."},
		{ID: 178, Text: "What does Performance Insights visualize?", Options: [4]string{"Database load", "Network traffic", "S3 access logs", "CloudFormation stacks"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Performance Insights monitors database performance. Visualizes database load. Identifies top SQL statements. No performance impact. Essential for performance tuning. Pay per vCPU hour. Helps optimize database performance. Easy to enable."},
		{ID: 179, Text: "What level of metrics does Enhanced Monitoring provide?", Options: [4]string{"OS-level metrics", "Only application metrics", "Only network metrics", "Only storage metrics"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Enhanced Monitoring provides OS-level metrics. CPU, memory, disk, network. More detailed than basic monitoring. CloudWatch integration. Essential for troubleshooting. Pay for CloudWatch Logs. Helps identify performance issues. Granular metrics (1-60 second intervals)."},
		{ID: 180, Text: "What does Event Subscription send?", Options: [4]string{"Notifications for RDS events", "Only emails", "Only SMS", "Only webhooks"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Event Subscription sends notifications for RDS events. Instance creation, deletion, failover, etc. SNS integration. Essential for monitoring. Free service. Helps track database changes. Can trigger automated responses."},
		{ID: 181, Text: "What does S3 Transfer Acceleration use?", Options: [4]string{"CloudFront edge locations", "Direct Connect", "VPN tunnels", "Satellite links"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Transfer Acceleration uses CloudFront edge locations for faster S3 uploads. Optimized path to S3. Reduces latency for long distances. Essential for global uploads. Pay for acceleration and data transfer. Can improve upload speed significantly. Uses edge network."},
		{ID: 182, Text: "What does Multipart Upload do?", Options: [4]string{"Uploads files in parallel parts", "Only compresses files", "Only encrypts files", "Only validates files"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Multipart Upload splits large files into parts. Uploads parts in parallel. Resumable if part fails. Essential for files > 5GB. More reliable than single upload. Faster for large files. Each part can be up to 5GB."},
		{ID: 183, Text: "What do Byte-Range Fetches download?", Options: [4]string{"Specific byte ranges", "Entire objects only", "Only metadata", "Only headers"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Byte-Range Fetches download specific byte ranges of objects. HTTP Range requests. Essential for partial downloads, video streaming, resuming downloads. More efficient than downloading entire object. Supports multiple ranges in single request."},
		{ID: 184, Text: "What does S3 Object Lock prevent?", Options: [4]string{"Object deletion or overwriting", "Only reads", "Only uploads", "Only tagging"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Object Lock prevents object deletion or overwriting. WORM (Write Once Read Many). Governance mode (can be overridden) or Compliance mode (cannot be overridden). Essential for compliance. Retention periods. Legal holds. Protects against accidental or malicious deletion."},
		{ID: 185, Text: "What does S3 Inventory provide?", Options: [4]string{"Scheduled reports of objects", "Only real-time monitoring", "Only access logs", "Only error logs"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "S3 Inventory provides scheduled reports of objects. Lists objects and metadata. CSV, ORC, Parquet formats. Essential for auditing, compliance, analytics. Can filter by prefix, tags. Delivered to S3 bucket. Helps manage large buckets."},
		{ID: 186, Text: "What does S3 Analytics analyze?", Options: [4]string{"Storage access patterns", "Only network traffic", "Only user logins", "Only API calls"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "S3 Analytics analyzes storage access patterns. Recommends lifecycle policies. Identifies infrequently accessed objects. Essential for cost optimization. Free service. Helps optimize storage costs. Data-driven lifecycle decisions."},
		{ID: 187, Text: "What scale does S3 Batch Operations handle?", Options: [4]string{"Millions of objects", "Only tens of objects", "Only hundreds of objects", "Only thousands of objects"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Batch Operations performs large-scale operations on S3 objects. Copy, invoke Lambda, restore from Glacier, etc. Processes millions of objects. Essential for bulk changes. Pay per operation. More efficient than individual API calls. Handles failures automatically."},
		{ID: 188, Text: "What does S3 Object Lambda do?", Options: [4]string{"Transforms objects on-the-fly", "Only stores objects", "Only deletes objects", "Only tags objects"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Object Lambda uses Lambda to transform objects on-the-fly. Modify data as it's retrieved. No need to store multiple copies. Essential for data transformation. Pay for Lambda execution and data transfer. Useful for filtering, redacting, converting formats."},
		{ID: 189, Text: "What do S3 Access Points provide?", Options: [4]string{"Shared access to S3 buckets", "Only backups", "Only monitoring", "Only logging"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Access Points provide shared access to S3 buckets. Unique hostname per access point. Different permissions per access point. Essential for multi-tenant applications. Simplifies access management. Can restrict by VPC, IP, IAM. Reduces bucket policy complexity."},
		{ID: 190, Text: "What do Multi-Region Access Points provide?", Options: [4]string{"Global endpoints for S3", "Only local access", "Only single region access", "Only cross-AZ access"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Multi-Region Access Points provide global endpoints for S3. Routes to nearest region automatically. Active-active setup. Reduces latency. Essential for global applications. Improves availability. Single endpoint for multiple regions."},
		{ID: 191, Text: "What does S3 Intelligent-Tiering do?", Options: [4]string{"Automatically optimizes storage costs", "Only encrypts data", "Only backs up data", "Only replicates data"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers. Monitors access patterns. No retrieval fees. Small monitoring fee per object. Essential for unknown access patterns. Optimizes costs automatically. Four access tiers: Frequent, Infrequent, Archive, Deep Archive."},
		{ID: 192, Text: "What retrieval time does Glacier Instant Retrieval provide?", Options: [4]string{"Milliseconds", "Hours", "Days", "Weeks"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Glacier Instant Retrieval provides archive storage with millisecond retrieval. Lower cost than Standard-IA. For rarely accessed data needing instant access. Essential for archive with instant access needs. Cheaper than Standard-IA for long-term storage."},
		{ID: 193, Text: "How many retrieval options does Glacier Flexible Retrieval have?", Options: [4]string{"Three (Expedited, Standard, Bulk)", "One", "Two", "Five"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Glacier Flexible Retrieval (formerly Glacier) provides low-cost archive storage. Three retrieval options: Expedited (1-5 min), Standard (3-5 hours), Bulk (5-12 hours). Essential for long-term archives. Cheapest option for infrequent access. Retrieval fees apply."},
		{ID: 194, Text: "What is the retrieval time for Glacier Deep Archive?", Options: [4]string{"12 hours", "1 hour", "1 minute", "Instant"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost storage class. 12-hour retrieval time. Essential for long-term archives, compliance data. Cheapest storage option. Retrieval takes 12 hours. Suitable for data rarely accessed."},
		{ID: 195, Text: "Who pays for data transfer with Requester Pays?", Options: [4]string{"The requester", "The bucket owner", "AWS", "No one"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Requester Pays makes the requester pay for data transfer and requests. Bucket owner pays only for storage. Essential for sharing large datasets. Reduces costs for data providers. Requesters need appropriate permissions."},
		{ID: 196, Text: "What do Presigned URLs provide?", Options: [4]string{"Temporary access to S3 objects", "Permanent access", "Only read access", "Only write access"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Presigned URLs provide temporary access to S3 objects. Time-limited (expires). No IAM credentials needed for requester. Essential for sharing private objects. Generated by bucket owner. Useful for downloads, uploads. Expires after specified time."},
		{ID: 197, Text: "What does CORS allow?", Options: [4]string{"Cross-origin web browser access", "Only same-origin access", "Only API access", "Only CLI access"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) allows web browsers to access S3 from different domains. Configures allowed origins, methods, headers. Essential for web applications accessing S3. Prevents unauthorized cross-origin access. Configured via bucket CORS policy."},
		{ID: 198, Text: "What can S3 host?", Options: [4]string{"Static websites", "Dynamic web applications", "Databases", "Email servers"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "S3 can host static websites. Configure index and error documents. Public read access needed. Essential for static websites. Low cost. Integrates with CloudFront for CDN. No servers to manage."},
		{ID: 199, Text: "What does MFA Delete require?", Options: [4]string{"MFA to delete object versions", "Only password", "Only API key", "Only IP whitelist"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "MFA Delete requires MFA to delete object versions or change versioning state. Extra protection for important data. Essential for compliance. Prevents accidental or malicious deletion. Only root account can enable. Adds security layer."},
		{ID: 200, Text: "What does Server Access Logging log?", Options: [4]string{"All requests to S3 bucket", "Only failed requests", "Only successful requests", "Only delete requests"}, CorrectAnswer: 0, Explanation: "Server Access Logging logs all requests to S3 bucket. Detailed access information. Stored in another S3 bucket. Essential for auditing and security. Helps track access patterns. Useful for compliance. Can analyze with Athena."},
	})
}
